<assembly xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v1" manifestVersion="1.0">
  <dependency>
    <dependentAssembly>
      <assemblyIdentity
        type="win32"
        name="Microsoft.Windows.Common-Controls"
        version="6.0.0.0"
        processorArchitecture="*"
        publicKeyToken="6595b64144ccf1df"
        language="*"
      />
    </dependentAssembly>
  </dependency>
  <!--
    Long paths (M8). `<data>\backups\<key>\<file name>\<stamp>-<file name>` passes the
    260-character MAX_PATH as soon as a CAM-generated file name gets long, and so does
    any program in a folder tree a shop nests deeply.

    Rust's own file calls already cope: `std::sys::path::windows::maybe_verbatim` puts
    `\\?\` in front of every absolute path of 248 characters or more before it reaches
    CreateFileW or CreateDirectoryW, so gEdit's reads and writes are not what this is
    for. What this is for is everything in the process that is *not* std — the common
    item dialog behind File ▸ Open among them — which takes the ordinary spelling and
    is held to MAX_PATH unless the process says otherwise.

    Microsoft ("Maximum Path Length Limitation") asks for both halves: this manifest
    entry, and the machine-wide policy. The policy is the user's to set, and
    `docs/user/README.md` says so under the backups.
  -->
  <application xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v3">
    <windowsSettings>
      <longPathAware xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2016/WindowsSettings">true</longPathAware>
    </windowsSettings>
  </application>
</assembly>
